1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,230
Heads, I riffle shuffle a sorted deck four times.

2
00:00:03,610 --> 00:00:06,340
Tails, a computer shuffles it perfectly.

3
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:09,780
From the deck alone, how often could you tell which?

4
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:13,280
Pick fifty, seventy five, or a hundred percent.

5
00:00:15,133 --> 00:00:19,493
Monte Carlo samplers move by random steps, like these riffles.

6
00:00:19,873 --> 00:00:23,493
Stop them too early, and they still remember where they started.

7
00:00:23,833 --> 00:00:27,303
The obvious check is the top card, the one you'd be dealt.

8
00:00:27,683 --> 00:00:31,503
Here's how often each card ends up there after four riffles.

9
00:00:31,866 --> 00:00:32,976
Almost flat.

10
00:00:33,356 --> 00:00:37,216
Only the old top card stands out: one time in fifteen.

11
00:00:37,596 --> 00:00:41,706
Judging by the top card, you'd be right fifty two percent of the time.

12
00:00:42,086 --> 00:00:44,046
Four riffles look like plenty.

13
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:47,280
A guesser who sees the whole deck does far better.

14
00:00:47,660 --> 00:00:53,050
The model cuts near the middle, then drops cards from each half in proportion to its size.

15
00:00:53,400 --> 00:01:01,860
Each half stays in order, so one riffle leaves two rising sequences: runs of consecutive cards, reading left to right.

16
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:04,120
Give each sequence its own level.

17
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:09,020
The cut crosses each level once, so a riffle can at most split it in two.

18
00:01:09,366 --> 00:01:10,456
Three riffles,

19
00:01:11,256 --> 00:01:11,906
eight.

20
00:01:12,286 --> 00:01:13,356
Four riffles,

21
00:01:13,956 --> 00:01:16,746
at most sixteen; this deck has fourteen.

22
00:01:17,100 --> 00:01:23,780
A perfect shuffle averages twenty six and a half, and gives sixteen or fewer once in two million decks.

23
00:01:24,133 --> 00:01:29,553
Call sixteen or fewer riffled, and you'd be wrong about once in four million games.

24
00:01:29,933 --> 00:01:33,873
The top card can't see this; it lives in the order between cards.

25
00:01:34,233 --> 00:01:40,123
Bayer and Diaconis proved that in this model, decks with the same count are equally likely.

26
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:43,120
So pile decks onto their counts,

27
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:46,900
then every possible deck, weighted by its chance.

28
00:01:47,266 --> 00:01:51,336
The red area, purple above blue, is the distance from random.

29
00:01:51,716 --> 00:01:52,946
Right now it's one.

30
00:01:53,300 --> 00:01:55,770
How many more riffles to bring it below a half?

31
00:01:57,633 --> 00:01:59,793
A fifth riffle: point nine two.

32
00:02:00,173 --> 00:02:02,003
A sixth: point six one.

33
00:02:02,383 --> 00:02:04,203
A seventh: point three three.

34
00:02:04,566 --> 00:02:13,086
Guess riffled where purple is higher: you catch sixty five percent of riffled decks, and flag thirty two percent of perfect ones.

35
00:02:13,466 --> 00:02:18,506
Accuracy is a half, plus half the difference, and the difference is the red area.

36
00:02:19,366 --> 00:02:25,326
Each riffle roughly halved the gap, but blue's spread, its standard deviation, stayed about two.

37
00:02:25,706 --> 00:02:29,586
While the gap spans several spreads, the distance stays near one.

38
00:02:29,966 --> 00:02:34,086
Within about one spread, it halves every riffle: that's the cliff.

39
00:02:34,433 --> 00:02:39,383
Multiply each gap by two to the k, and it settles near two hundred thirty.

40
00:02:40,583 --> 00:02:45,853
Divide that by two each riffle, and it reaches one spread after just under seven.

41
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:56,280
For n cards, two hundred thirty grows like n squared, but the spread only like root n, so the cliff sits near three halves log base two of n.

42
00:02:56,633 --> 00:02:58,143
Aldous proved that law.

43
00:02:58,543 --> 00:03:06,763
Bayer and Diaconis computed the exact curve: for fifty two cards, seven riffles is the first count below a half.

44
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:15,200
Trefethen and Trefethen measured information instead: no cliff, and five or six shuffles do a reasonable job.

45
00:03:15,533 --> 00:03:25,383
Many Markov chains share this cliff, and samplers share the trap: one statistic can settle, like the top card, long before the whole chain has.

46
00:03:25,733 --> 00:03:27,153
Back to the coin flip.

47
00:03:27,533 --> 00:03:32,693
After four riffles you'd almost never be wrong, same as with an unshuffled deck.

48
00:03:33,033 --> 00:03:34,343
Three more riffles,

49
00:03:35,543 --> 00:03:38,013
and you'd be right only two times out of three.

50
00:03:38,366 --> 00:03:41,276
Four riffles fool someone watching the top card.

51
00:03:41,656 --> 00:03:45,266
Fooling someone who counts sequences that well takes ten.

